---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Input Perturbation as Augmentation for Pretraining Distillation — Jack Cai"
  description="Distilling on perturbed inputs for part of pretraining improves small language models, and the gain comes from the teacher's predictions on the perturbed text."
>
  <section class="page-intro">
    <h1>Input Perturbation as Augmentation for Pretraining Distillation</h1>
    <p>Ziyang Cai · October 2026 · <a class="pdf-link" href="/writing/input-perturbation-distillation.pdf">Paper (PDF)</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="post-body">
    <p>
      Knowledge distillation trains a student language model to match a teacher's predictions on
      the training text. We ask whether the student also benefits from the teacher's predictions on
      text that has been deliberately perturbed. For an early part of pretraining we perturb the
      inputs and train the student to match the teacher on the perturbed text, and we spend the rest
      of the same token budget on ordinary distillation. Teacher and student usually have embeddings
      of different sizes, so we perturb the one-hot token vectors, which each model then maps through
      its own embedding table. We test three perturbations: additive token noise, blending two
      documents, and blending neighbouring tokens.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <a href="/writing/input-perturbation-method.png"><img src="/writing/input-perturbation-method.png" alt="The three perturbation families, noise, parallel blend and adjacent blend, shown on a toy vocabulary." /></a>
    </figure>
    <p>
      When pretraining from scratch, the method improves on standard distillation at both scales we
      tried, by 0.025 nats at 135M parameters and by 0.020 nats at 0.5B (three seeds). At 135M this
      is 1.45 times the gain that distillation itself provides over next-token training. The gain
      depends on the teacher seeing the perturbed text: without a teacher it largely disappears, and
      perturbing only the student's input, as pretraining augmentation usually does, recovers about a
      third of it, while softer targets and a weaker early teacher do not reproduce it. When 262M
      unique tokens are repeated for 16 epochs, the gain persists against a baseline with tuned
      weight decay. The perturbed students also quantize at least as well with GPTQ and AWQ, and at
      0.5B they gain about one point of average zero-shot accuracy over seven tasks.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <a href="/writing/input-perturbation-results.png"><img src="/writing/input-perturbation-results.png" alt="Change in final validation loss against standard distillation for each perturbation family at 135M and 0.5B." /></a>
      <figcaption>Change in final validation loss against standard distillation (below zero is better), at 135M (top) and 0.5B (bottom).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><a class="pdf-link" href="/writing/input-perturbation-distillation.pdf">Read the full paper (PDF) →</a></p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

